WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.950
Dominoes, Quick Starter, Lisa's Song by Leslie Thompson.

00:06.000 --> 00:10.830
Read by Richard Linson, Chris Mills, Penny Rawlins,

00:10.880 --> 00:16.230
Jane Whittenshaw, James Daniel Wilson, and Jennifer Woodwood.

00:16.280 --> 00:21.910
Published in copyright, Oxford University Press 2012.

00:21.960 --> 00:25.350
Chapter 1, Al's First Love.

00:25.400 --> 00:35.350
MUSIC

00:35.400 --> 00:38.790
Al Brown is 13.

00:38.840 --> 00:43.950
He lives with his parents in a little house.

00:44.000 --> 00:47.950
It's in Nottingham in England.

00:48.000 --> 00:54.550
The family are happy, but they have not got much money.

00:54.600 --> 00:58.710
So life is not easy.

00:58.760 --> 01:06.350
When our story begins, Mrs. Brown is expecting a baby.

01:06.400 --> 01:13.390
Al is waiting excitedly for his new little brother or sister.

01:13.440 --> 01:17.630
Al's first love is music.

01:17.680 --> 01:22.790
He can play the guitar very well.

01:22.840 --> 01:29.750
He is in a band, fast cars, with three of his friends.

01:29.800 --> 01:35.950
They are Emma, Mick, and Ben.

01:36.000 --> 01:41.230
Ben and Al play the guitars in the band.

01:41.280 --> 01:49.320
Emma sings, and Mick plays the drums.

01:49.760 --> 01:53.670
Al writes songs for the band.

01:53.720 --> 01:57.310
They play at Ben's house.

01:57.360 --> 02:02.830
Al's parents often come and listen to them.

02:02.880 --> 02:07.550
They are good friends with Ben's parents.

02:07.600 --> 02:11.630
Our boy can't live without his music.

02:11.680 --> 02:14.470
Al's parents tell everyone.

02:14.520 --> 02:26.470
MUSIC

02:26.520 --> 02:30.470
Chapter 2. Al's Baby Sister.

02:30.520 --> 02:40.470
MUSIC

02:40.520 --> 02:46.870
One morning, Mrs. Brown does not feel well.

02:46.920 --> 02:51.270
The baby's coming, she says.

02:51.320 --> 02:57.070
What, it's six weeks early. Mr. Brown cries.

02:57.120 --> 02:59.870
Yes, I know.

02:59.920 --> 03:03.470
But please, take me to the hospital.

03:03.520 --> 03:07.070
This baby can't wait.

03:07.120 --> 03:10.670
Mrs. Brown is right.

03:10.720 --> 03:16.390
After six hours, Lisa arrives.

03:16.440 --> 03:21.950
She is beautiful, but she is very little.

03:22.000 --> 03:28.470
She must stay in hospital for two weeks.

03:28.520 --> 03:34.320
When Lisa comes home, life is not the same.

03:34.400 --> 03:40.710
The baby cries a lot, and she doesn't eat much.

03:40.760 --> 03:45.590
Is the little girl all right?

03:45.640 --> 03:48.990
Everyone is tired.

03:49.040 --> 03:58.670
When Al tries to play his guitar, his mother says, Shh, Lisa's sleeping.

03:58.720 --> 04:04.550
Mr. Brown says, You must help more in the house, Al.

04:04.600 --> 04:07.750
Your mother's very tired.

04:07.800 --> 04:13.350
Al tries to help, but he is tired too.

04:13.400 --> 04:22.550
He can't concentrate at school, and he forgets to do his homework.

04:22.600 --> 04:29.150
One day, Al visits his grandfather, Jack Brown.

04:29.200 --> 04:32.750
The old man lives alone.

04:32.800 --> 04:38.550
Al says. Mum and Dad never listen to me now.

04:38.600 --> 04:42.350
They're always with Lisa.

04:42.400 --> 04:46.950
A new baby is a lot of work.

04:47.000 --> 04:52.950
Jack says, You must understand that, Al.

04:53.000 --> 04:55.550
But what about you?

04:55.600 --> 04:58.750
Are you writing any new songs?

04:58.800 --> 05:02.550
You're a good songwriter.

05:02.600 --> 05:06.550
Al says nothing to his grandfather.

05:06.600 --> 05:12.350
He has no time for the band these days.

05:12.400 --> 05:18.750
When Jack is alone, he takes out an old photograph.

05:18.800 --> 05:21.150
He looks at it.

05:21.200 --> 05:26.800
It is a picture of his sister, Alice.

05:27.000 --> 05:32.550
Life's short, Al. He says quietly.

05:32.600 --> 05:35.950
Be good to your sister.

05:36.000 --> 05:41.950
You're older, but you don't always know best.

05:42.000 --> 05:55.950
CHAPTER 3

05:56.000 --> 06:07.950
AL'S ANGRY FRIENDS

06:08.000 --> 06:13.950
Al's friends are waiting for him at Ben's house.

06:14.000 --> 06:17.950
Al is late again.

06:18.000 --> 06:21.950
Where is he? Emma asks.

06:22.000 --> 06:25.950
We can't begin playing without him.

06:26.000 --> 06:31.950
He's always late these days. Ben says angrily.

06:32.000 --> 06:34.950
And he can't concentrate.

06:35.000 --> 06:38.950
Let's look for a different guitar player.

06:39.000 --> 06:41.950
My brother wants to be in the band.

06:42.000 --> 06:44.950
You're right, says Mick.

06:45.000 --> 06:47.950
Let's find someone new.

06:48.000 --> 06:52.950
Emma does not want to lose Al.

06:53.000 --> 06:56.950
She calls him on her mobile phone.

06:57.000 --> 06:59.950
Where are you?

07:00.000 --> 07:01.950
We're waiting for you.

07:02.000 --> 07:05.950
Get here fast and say sorry.

07:06.000 --> 07:10.950
Then perhaps we can forget these last weeks.

07:11.000 --> 07:13.950
But you must change, Al.

07:14.000 --> 07:18.950
You must give more time to the band.

07:19.000 --> 07:23.950
Al is in an internet cafe.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.950
He is trying to do his homework.

07:28.000 --> 07:32.950
Emma, things aren't easy for me these days.

07:33.000 --> 07:35.950
I can't work at home.

07:36.000 --> 07:38.950
Lisa's always crying.

07:39.000 --> 07:42.950
My parents are always with her.

07:43.000 --> 07:45.950
They tell me, do this.

07:46.000 --> 07:47.950
Do that.

07:48.000 --> 07:50.950
Now you're doing the same.

07:51.000 --> 07:53.950
Leave me alone.

07:54.000 --> 07:59.950
And he stops the call and puts the phone down.

08:00.000 --> 08:03.950
Emma is very angry.

08:04.000 --> 08:10.950
I'm trying to help Al, but he's not interested. She thinks.

08:11.000 --> 08:14.950
She quickly sends him a phone message.

08:15.000 --> 08:20.000
Fast cars is looking for a new guitar player.

08:21.000 --> 08:23.950
Don't worry about us.

08:24.000 --> 08:26.950
We don't need you anymore, Al.

08:27.000 --> 08:29.950
Goodbye.

08:30.000 --> 08:35.950
Al tries to phone Emma, but she doesn't answer.

08:36.000 --> 08:37.950
How does he feel?

08:38.000 --> 08:39.950
Bad.

08:40.000 --> 08:43.950
He does not want to lose his friends.

08:44.000 --> 08:47.950
And he loves playing in the band.

08:48.000 --> 08:50.950
But what can he do now?

08:51.000 --> 08:55.950
Just then, his phone rings again.

08:56.000 --> 08:58.950
This time, it's his grandfather.

08:59.000 --> 09:12.950
Chapter 4.

09:13.000 --> 09:16.950
Something is wrong.

09:17.000 --> 09:34.950
Al does not answer his mobile, and his grandfather does not leave a message for him.

09:35.000 --> 09:40.950
I can phone him later. Al thinks.

09:41.000 --> 09:45.950
When Al goes home, something is wrong.

09:46.000 --> 09:53.950
His mother's eyes are red, and his father's face is white.

09:54.000 --> 09:57.950
What's the matter? Al asks.

09:58.000 --> 10:01.950
It's your sister. Dad says.

10:02.000 --> 10:03.950
She's ill.

10:04.000 --> 10:05.950
Ill?

10:06.000 --> 10:08.950
Her heart isn't right, Al.

10:09.000 --> 10:13.000
She needs an operation, and quickly.

10:14.000 --> 10:15.950
There's more.

10:16.000 --> 10:18.950
It's a difficult operation.

10:19.000 --> 10:21.950
No hospital in England can do it.

10:22.000 --> 10:26.950
Lisa needs to go to the United States for it.

10:27.000 --> 10:28.950
I see.

10:29.000 --> 10:31.950
So, when are you going?

10:32.000 --> 10:33.950
We don't know.

10:34.000 --> 10:36.950
The operation's expensive.

10:37.000 --> 10:41.950
And there are the plane tickets and the hotel, too?

10:42.000 --> 10:44.950
Mr. Brown tries to smile.

10:45.000 --> 10:46.950
But we can find the money.

10:47.000 --> 10:49.950
Don't worry about it.

10:50.000 --> 10:54.950
Now Al is very sad and alone.

10:55.000 --> 10:58.950
Lisa is very ill, he knows.

10:59.000 --> 11:02.950
Perhaps he can phone Emma.

11:03.000 --> 11:10.950
Then, on his mobile, he sees a message from his grandfather.

11:11.000 --> 11:13.950
Come and see me.

11:14.000 --> 11:17.950
I know about Lisa.

11:18.000 --> 11:21.950
Jack is waiting for Al.

11:22.000 --> 11:28.950
He is thinking about his sister, Alice, dead at 13.

11:29.000 --> 11:32.950
Suddenly, Al runs in.

11:33.000 --> 11:36.950
Granddad, what can we do?

11:37.000 --> 11:41.950
Where can we find the money for Lisa's operation?

11:42.000 --> 11:46.950
Take it easy, Al. Says Granddad.

11:47.000 --> 11:49.950
I've got an idea.

11:50.000 --> 11:57.950
Tell your friends in the band, write a song for Lisa.

11:58.000 --> 12:00.950
A song for Lisa?

12:01.000 --> 12:02.950
How can that help?

12:03.000 --> 12:05.950
Trust me, Al.

12:06.000 --> 12:07.950
It can.

12:08.000 --> 12:13.000
Go home now and write your song.

12:34.000 --> 12:42.950
Back home, Al talks to his parents.

12:43.000 --> 12:45.950
I want to write a song for Lisa.

12:46.000 --> 12:49.950
I want to tell everyone about her.

12:50.000 --> 12:52.950
Mr. and Mrs. Brown smile.

12:53.000 --> 12:57.950
What a nice idea. His mum says.

12:58.000 --> 13:00.950
Thanks, Al.

13:01.000 --> 13:03.950
Al phones his friends.

13:04.000 --> 13:06.950
He meets them at Ben's house.

13:07.000 --> 13:09.950
He says sorry.

13:10.000 --> 13:15.950
Then, he tells them all about Lisa and the song.

13:16.000 --> 13:20.950
Emma looks at Mick and Ben.

13:21.000 --> 13:23.950
What do you think, boys?

13:24.000 --> 13:28.950
Can Al come back to the band now? She asks.

13:29.000 --> 13:32.950
Yes. Ben laughs.

13:33.000 --> 13:34.950
We need him.

13:35.000 --> 13:38.950
My brother can't play the guitar at all.

13:39.000 --> 13:43.950
And he can't write songs. Mick says.

13:44.000 --> 13:47.950
I've got an idea. Emma says.

13:48.000 --> 13:52.950
We can play Al's new song at our gig.

13:53.000 --> 13:55.000
What gig?

13:56.000 --> 13:59.950
The gig for Lisa. Emma says.

14:00.000 --> 14:02.950
People can pay at the door.

14:03.000 --> 14:06.950
With their help, Lisa can have her operation.

14:07.000 --> 14:11.950
Good idea. Ben cries excitedly.

14:12.000 --> 14:16.950
Let's tell people about it on our website.

14:17.000 --> 14:26.950
So Mick puts the day and time of their next gig up on the band's website.

14:27.000 --> 14:35.950
Two weeks later, after lots of work, the band plays at Al's school.

14:36.000 --> 14:39.950
Al's family and friends are there.

14:40.000 --> 14:46.950
Late in the evening, Al sings Lisa's song.

14:47.000 --> 14:50.950
Some people from the radio are there too.

14:51.000 --> 14:53.000
They record the song.

15:02.000 --> 15:04.950
Let's sing for Lisa and her life.

15:05.000 --> 15:06.950
The future's in her eyes are blue.

15:07.000 --> 15:09.950
A bright tomorrow's endless smile.

15:10.000 --> 15:14.000
So let's all help those times come true.

15:22.000 --> 15:30.950
Next day, Al and his friends look at the money from the gig.

15:31.000 --> 15:33.950
How much is there?

15:34.000 --> 15:37.950
Can Lisa have her operation now?

15:38.000 --> 15:51.950
Chapter 6

15:52.000 --> 15:53.950
Past concerts.

15:54.000 --> 15:55.950
Future gigs.

15:56.000 --> 16:08.950
We need more money. Al says.

16:09.000 --> 16:15.950
Then let's put Lisa's song on our website. Ben cries.

16:16.000 --> 16:19.950
People can pay for it and download it.

16:20.000 --> 16:24.950
People hear about Lisa's song on the radio.

16:25.000 --> 16:29.950
They pay and download it from the website.

16:30.000 --> 16:36.950
Soon, Al's family have the money for America.

16:37.000 --> 16:43.950
At the airport, Al and his grandfather say goodbye.

16:44.000 --> 16:46.950
Good luck!

16:47.000 --> 16:51.950
Al calls to his parents and Lisa.

16:52.000 --> 16:58.950
Suddenly, Jack remembers a night in 1941.

16:59.000 --> 17:01.950
His mother is dead.

17:02.000 --> 17:05.950
His father is away at the war.

17:06.000 --> 17:09.950
He is the man of the house now.

17:10.000 --> 17:16.950
Night after night, planes drop their bombs on their town.

17:17.000 --> 17:19.950
People are afraid.

17:20.000 --> 17:27.950
His sister, Alice, wants to sing and dance and forget the war.

17:28.000 --> 17:32.950
He hears her words that night and his answer.

17:33.000 --> 17:35.950
Please, can I go out, Jack?

17:36.000 --> 17:38.950
I want to sing at the concert.

17:39.000 --> 17:41.950
My friends are waiting.

17:42.000 --> 17:43.950
No, Alice.

17:44.000 --> 17:46.950
You're 13.

17:47.000 --> 17:49.950
You must stay at home.

17:50.000 --> 17:54.950
Later, he is working at the hospital.

17:55.000 --> 17:56.950
Bombs are dropping.

17:57.000 --> 17:59.950
The sky is red.

18:00.000 --> 18:06.950
Then someone says, Jack, there's a bomb in your street.

18:07.000 --> 18:09.950
Your house is on fire.

18:10.000 --> 18:13.950
Alice? He cries.

18:14.000 --> 18:16.950
He runs home.

18:17.000 --> 18:19.950
But he can do nothing.

18:20.000 --> 18:24.950
He never sees Alice again.

18:25.000 --> 18:30.950
What's the matter, Grandad? Al asks.

18:31.000 --> 18:33.950
Jack smiles at his grandson.

18:34.000 --> 18:38.950
He sees a lot of Alice in the boy.

18:39.000 --> 18:43.950
Sometimes, past experience teaches you things.

18:44.000 --> 18:49.950
Oh, it's not important now.

18:50.000 --> 18:54.950
But you must write some more songs.

18:55.000 --> 18:57.950
When's the band's next concert?

18:58.000 --> 19:00.950
Er, gig.

19:01.000 --> 19:02.950
Soon, Grandad.

19:03.000 --> 19:11.000
With that, Grandfather and grandson, leave the airport and go home.
